    "body": "A section of the Abitibi Greenstone Belt. Credit: The Northern Miner.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 19, 2026\nEpic Gold (CSE: EPG; US-OTC: NFLDF) has established its first gold resources at the Wilson and Fenton properties in Quebec’s Abitibi greenstone belt, giving the explorer a basis for expansion drilling at both projects.\nWilson holds an estimated 279,000 inferred oz. gold, compared with 90,000 inferred oz. for Fenton, Epic said Thursday. Nearly three-quarters of Wilson’s gold falls within conceptual open-pit reporting limits.\nLocated 15 km east of Lebel-sur-Quévillon, Wilson has inferred resources of 3.05 million tonnes at 2.85 grams gold per tonne. Of that, 2.41 million tonnes at 2.66 grams for 205,000 oz. sit within the projected open pit, while the underground portion holds 642,000 inferred tonnes at 3.57 grams for 74,000 ounces.\nWilson’s initial resource “provides a meaningful starting point for evaluating the project,” CEO Rod Husband said in the statement. “Importantly, the estimate incorporates both open-pit and underground reporting constraints, providing a framework for understanding the distribution of mineralization and assessing future exploration priorities.”\nAs for Fenton, Epic’s geological interpretation “indicates that mineralization extends beyond the current resource constraints, highlighting opportunities for further discovery,” said exploration vice-president Mark Richardson.\nFenton is estimated to hold inferred resources of 1.92 million tonnes at 1.46 grams gold per tonne. Both estimates, effective Nov. 9, used a long-term gold price of $3,600 (C$5,148) an oz., assumed 92% recovery and drew on historical drilling. The figures come a day after Epic disclosed an update resource for its third Quebec project, Benoist, which put indicated gold at 150,700 oz. and inferred gold at 86,900 ounces.\nFormer Exploits\nFormerly named Exploits Discovery, the company adopted the Epic name earlier this year. Its shares began trading Feb. 18 as it focused on a portfolio spanning Ontario and Quebec.\nThe Quebec properties came through a 2025 option agreement with Cartier Resources (TSXV: ECR) to earn up to a 100% interest in Wilson, Fenton and Benoist through cash payments, share issuances and exploration spending.\nAt Fenton, Epic completed 4,259 metres of drilling in 10 holes this year. Results released in May included 5.85 metres grading 4.65 grams gold per tonne at the Everest target and 76.8 metres at 1.23 grams at Fenton Main. The intervals are drilled lengths; true widths remain unknown.\nEpic also reported a current resource at its optioned Hawkins project in Ontario in October: about 14 million inferred tonnes grading 0.98 gram gold per tonne for 440,000 oz. The McKinnon deposit lies about 80 km south-southwest of Hearst. Together with Benoist, Wilson and Fenton, it gives Epic current resource estimates at all four projects.\nShares of Epic dropped 9.5% to C19¢ Thursday afternoon, valuing the company at about C$12.7 million ($8.9 million). The stock has traded between C16¢ and C38¢ since Epic went public.\nPrint\nTAG: gold gold exploration gold projects Quebec exploration\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: Epic Gold\nRepublish this article",
